::GEO_U06_L06_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>If k = 4, how do lengths change? {
=A. They become 4 times as large#Linear measurements change by k.
~B. They become 16 times as large#That is the area or surface area effect.
~C. They become 64 times as large#That is the volume effect.
~D. They become 8 times as large#This doubles k instead of using k.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>If k = 4, how does area change? {
~A. 4 times#That is the length effect.
=B. 16 times#Area changes by k&sup2;, so 4&sup2;=16.
~C. 64 times#That is the volume effect.
~D. 8 times#This does not square k.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>If k = 4, how does volume change? {
~A. 4 times#That is the length effect.
~B. 16 times#That is the area effect.
=C. 64 times#Volume changes by k&sup3;, so 4&sup3;=64.
~D. 12 times#Multiplying by 3 is not the volume rule.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A similar solid has volume scale factor 27. What is the linear scale factor? {
~A. 27#That is the volume scale factor, not the linear scale factor.
~B. 9#This is not the cube root of 27.
~C. 6#This does not match k&sup3;=27.
=D. 3#Since 3&sup3;=27, the linear scale factor is 3.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is true for scale factor k? {
=A. Area changes by k&sup2; and volume changes by k&sup3;#Area is two-dimensional and volume is three-dimensional.
~B. Area changes by k and volume changes by k&sup2;#This shifts each exponent too low.
~C. All measurements change by k#Only linear measurements change by k.
~D. Volume changes by k&sup2;#Volume changes by k&sup3;.
}

